    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring question: does this government actually have the legal authority and the money to do what it is being asked to do. Dillon's Rule and home rule set the baseline legal authority every other concept in this lecture operates within, and preemption is the reminder that even broad home rule authority remains a grant the state can narrow at any time. The property tax and its tax-and-expenditure limitations, intergovernmental grants and the unfunded mandate problem, and school-finance equalization together describe where a local government's money actually comes from and why an identical dollar figure can mean something very different depending on a city's own tax base and its state's specific formula. Special districts and the fragmentation they create, and the choice between mayor-council and council-manager government, both shape who is actually accountable for a given decision once authority and money are both in place. Annexation and consolidation describe how jurisdictional boundaries themselves shift over time, often in direct response to the fiscal and authority pressures covered earlier, and incentive competition shows what happens when thousands of separately governed jurisdictions chase the same mobile investment at once. Detroit's bankruptcy closes the lecture by showing nearly all nine earlier ideas converging on a single real city: an eroded revenue base, a state willing to override elected local control, and a negotiated settlement that needed outside money no purely local fix could have assembled. As you follow any real local government story from here forward, ask first what specific legal authority is actually in play, then where the money would have to come from, and you will usually find the rest of the story follows from those two answers.",
