        explanation:
          "Now turn to the everyday mechanics of running a Spanish municipio once its comunidad and provincial relationships are understood. The alcalde is elected, much like the French maire covered in this course's French lecture, not directly by residents but by the newly elected pleno, the municipal council, from among its own members immediately following each municipal election, typically resulting in the leading list's own candidate becoming alcalde when that list secures an outright majority, though Spain's proportional municipal electoral system frequently produces no single majority list, requiring a negotiated pacto de investidura among multiple parties' councillors to actually install an alcalde, a coalition-formation dynamic directly comparable to the national-level government-formation bargaining covered in this course's Domestic Policy lecture, just playing out at municipal scale instead [7]. Once installed, an alcalde governing without a reliable pleno majority faces a genuine, recurring risk unique among this course's local-government lectures: a formal moción de censura, a motion of no confidence that a new majority coalition of councillors can use to remove a sitting alcalde mid-term and install a different one without requiring a fresh municipal election, a mechanism with real practical teeth that has unseated sitting alcaldes in numerous Spanish municipios over the years whenever a governing coalition's internal arithmetic shifted.",
        whyItMatters:
          "You should treat the pacto de investidura and the moción de censura together as the reason Spanish municipal politics can carry real, ongoing coalition-stability risk throughout an alcalde's full term, not just at the moment of initial election, a genuinely different risk profile from the French system covered in this course's French lecture, where the maire's position is comparatively more secure once installed, and from the US council-manager or mayor-council systems, where an American mayor generally cannot be removed mid-term through an equivalent council-only mechanism. For anyone advising a Spanish alcalde governing without a solid single-party majority, continuously monitoring the pleno's actual coalition arithmetic, not just at inauguration but throughout the term, is a genuinely necessary, ongoing task rather than a one-time check after the election results come in.",
        example:
          "Numerous Spanish municipios, including several provincial capitals, have seen a sitting alcalde removed mid-term through a successful moción de censura when a shifting coalition of opposition councillors assembled the necessary majority, a mechanism Spanish local-politics commentary treats as a routine, if still politically dramatic, feature of municipal governance rather than an exceptional crisis event. Spain's proportional municipal electoral system means even mid-sized municipios frequently require a multi-party pacto de investidura to install an alcalde at all after an election, making genuine multi-party coalition management, not just campaign strategy, a central, ongoing skill for any serious Spanish local political figure to master.",
